 wrong I don't know but it's been my  perspective I was lucky enough to go to  China in 1984 so 35 years ago as a guest  of acidic and which was the only window  company at the time that was the only  company that could look at the outside  world and deal with the outside world  you know at that time I would bring as a  gift  $10 calculators to people who ran  companies and they thought they were  miraculous machines and I remember  sitting us giving a lecture to teach  them about the financial markets on the  10th floor of the chocolate building and  to look out on the chocolate building  and there were all the hutongs that were  down there and then to imagine what  China would be like because China was  opening up so I could see that the world  was here in cost and this and China was  here and there was going to be an  opening up in the world would change as  a result of that opening up so I looked  out on the hutongs and when I looked at  the hutongs and said this is going to  change you know they said you don't know  China and I said no cave I don't know  that there's globalization and who knows  what would  and since then china's income levels  have increased by twenty six times its  share of world GDP went from two percent  to 22 percent now on its poverty rate  went from over eighty eight percent to  less than one percent its life  expectancy increased by ten years  there's no greater miracle than has  happened in China not been in the  fortunate position to know the Chinese  people many people who have become  thirty-year type of friendships and then  I was able to see things and so I can  only describe what it is through my eyes  it's to me the perspectives are  different and one of the leaders  described to me that the essence of that  difference as in the United States but  the United States is a country of  individuals and individualism and it  treasures the individual it treasures  the revolutionary individual the Steve  Jobs type of personality who will  disrupt the system and so on and in  China it is the family and as an  extension of the family the collective  and he described that the word country  consists of two words - two characters  which is state family and that there's a  top-down system and there were different  core values as much in that's the same  but there is also a different  perspective and that perspective then  gets reflected in some of the  differences in the conflicts that we see  the issue is the ability to see things  through each other's eyes I've been very  lucky to be dealing with particularly  economic policymakers so that I could  see things through their eyes and of  course economics has also shifted to  geopolitics and by being able to see  things through their eyes I would say  that by and large I would be doing the  same sort of economic policies that  they've done and that if I was to look  for  the long-term in terms of productivity  and the like and the things that are  happening in the way of innovation that  entrepreneurship creativity in these  types of things the reforms but we're  going through a particular period of  time to where there was a debt  adjustment and how to manage that  sometimes it seems that a that when you  do healthy things in coentrão and  constraining the debt that it is a lot  less fun and a lot more painful than  when you have a great time getting into  debt so there are these types of  adjustments that are that that are being  made as I look at this my responsibility  is a an economic investor a global Mecca  nama kin Wester is to try to find what  are the elements that make countries  succeed and fail and to do that over  time  and that's led me to develop indicators  and literally quantify those indicators  into leading economic indicators for the  well-being in my study of watching why  reserve currencies and the countries  behind them have risen and declined with  the help of my fabulous research team  we've been able to look through times  those are arcs those along arcs in other  words there's the US as the existing  world's reserve currency before that  there was the British Empire before that  there was the Dutch Empire and these  things take place over long periods of  time a hundred two hundred years many of  those we can't see but yet they take us  by surprise like the change in the  reserve currency and the factors that we  see over a period of time in terms of  the indicators that we put together are  I'll describe it and brief indicators  first of education and civility the  better the education and the more the  civility of the behavior of people  together infrastructure infrastructure  is a good leading indicator and then  technology the invention of new  technologies technology  is not only a commercial asset it is a  military asset the capacity to build  technology is leading indicator for  example you know when the Dutch invented  ships that can go all around the world  and they carried guns and because in  Europe they fought a lot then they were  able to account for half of world trade  Holland because of the importance of  technology then you get improvements in  output GDP Rises and then you have  larger and larger shares of world trade  it's a reflection of the competitiveness  of how that arises over a period of time  because you grow because you're  competitive and you raise those living  standards and as you go globally it's  the development of the military because  you need the military to both protect  your trade routes and also to protect  the natural resources and then with that  there's always the development of a  financial system the financial hub you  know in the Dutch Empire was Amsterdam  then it was London then it was New York  and increasingly it will be Shanghai and  this is an evolution and with that  there's the development of a reserve  currency because it is the medium by  which there is a common currency and a  common exchange rate and when that  exists it also means that those  countries can get deeper into debt  because everyone wants to save in the  currency and you set your lent that  currency and then they get deeper in  debt and the economies get overextended  and that is sort of the history of the  rise and declines of vampires and  there's a certain destiny that happens  as a result of that and I want to share  with you just a couple of slides this  first chart shows that these indicators  applied to all the major empires  starting around 1500 so you could see  the rises and declines of each of those  empires this is the average of these  indicators and I think it helps to give  me perspective and I hope it gives  perspective because you can see that red  line is the red line of China and what  you see is not only a rise  at a strong pace but you also see where  China was in the relative importance  throughout history China was for most of  its history the most important or one of  the most important empires in the world  for ahead of Europe inventing the  printing press 500 years ahead and so on  and it is because of an approach it is  not an American approach it's not  democracy it's not but it is an approach  it's their approach a Confucian approach  it has to do with the way that they're  operating and so there are things that  make Americans American and there are  things that make Chinese Chinese and we  can't ever expect that we're going to  make the Chinese like Americans and to  adopt our system any more than they  should expect that Americans should be  made like Chinese and so as we have that  type of competition I'll I'll take you  back just we took it back to the Tong  dynasty so there's a tremendous culture  and a tremendous capability in history  that has to do with what what they exist  and so it is with great admiration I  have great admiration for the Chinese a  different system and with that on there  are Wars we you I don't like the word  war rather than negotiations but there  is you know there's a 